 <li><strong>Fiber.</strong> A meta-analysis of 16 randomized trials (1,251 adults) found 66% responded to fiber supplements against 41% on control. Psyllium was among the fibers with a clear effect, and benefit appeared only at doses above 10 g a day.</li>
 <li><strong>Magnesium oxide.</strong> In a small placebo-controlled trial (34 women, 1.5 g a day for 28 days), 70.6% improved on magnesium oxide against 25.0% on placebo.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>The 2023 joint guideline from the American Gastroenterological Association and American College of Gastroenterology gives conditional recommendations for fiber and magnesium oxide in chronic constipation, and a strong recommendation for polyethylene glycol, an over-the-counter laxative that is not a supplement.</p>
 <p><strong>Cautions.</strong> Fiber can add to bloating, which GLP-1 drugs already cause in some people. Magnesium is cleared by the kidneys, so people with reduced kidney function should not use magnesium laxatives without medical advice. Severe or persistent constipation, or constipation with vomiting and abdominal pain, needs prompt medical attention, not a supplement. More on forms and safety is on our <a href="/ingredients/magnesium">magnesium evidence page</a>.</p>
 
 <h2>Hair shedding: no supplement has been shown to help</h2>
 <p>A 2026 systematic review of 24 studies found hair loss reported more often with semaglutide and tirzepatide than with older GLP-1 drugs. Rapid weight loss appeared to be a contributor, and telogen effluvium, a temporary shedding that follows physical stress, was one of the main types reported.</p>
 <p>Biotin is the supplement most often marketed for this. A systematic review of biotin for hair and nails found only 18 reported cases of benefit, and every one involved a person with an underlying deficiency or medical cause. Evidence in otherwise healthy people is limited. We found no study of biotin or collagen for hair shedding during GLP-1 treatment.</p>
 
 <h2>Supplements that need care with GLP-1 drugs</h2>
 <ul>
 <li><strong>Anything that lowers blood sugar.</strong> <a href="/ingredients/berberine">Berberine</a> lowers glucose in people with type 2 diabetes. The Wegovy label warns of low blood sugar when semaglutide is combined with insulin or sulfonylureas. No trial has tested berberine together with a GLP-1 drug, so tell your prescriber before combining them, especially if you take other diabetes medication. See <a href="/glp-1/berberine-vs-ozempic">berberine vs Ozempic</a>.</li>
 <li><strong>Anything taken by mouth on a schedule.</strong> The label notes that semaglutide delays stomach emptying and may affect the absorption of oral medications. Whether that changes how any supplement is absorbed has not been studied.</li>
 <li><strong>Laxative herbs.</strong> Diarrhea and vomiting are already common on these drugs, and the label warns that the resulting fluid loss can injure the kidneys. Stimulant laxative herbs such as <a href="/ingredients/senna">senna</a> can add to fluid loss.</li>
 </ul>
 
 <h2>What we found no evidence for</h2>
 <ul>
 <li><strong>Electrolyte products for people on GLP-1 drugs.</strong> We found no clinical study. Staying hydrated matters when vomiting or diarrhea occurs, but that is not evidence for a specific product.</li>
 <li><strong>"GLP-1 support" blends</strong> and supplements claimed to raise the body's own GLP-1. We found no randomized trial of any such product alongside these drugs.</li>
 <li><strong>Collagen for muscle or hair during GLP-1 treatment.</strong> Not studied.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>Vitamin B12 mixed into compounded semaglutide is covered separately: see <a href="/glp-1/semaglutide-with-b12">semaglutide with B12</a>.</p>
 
