+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Tirzepatide vs Semaglutide: What Head-to-Head Trials Show | Supplement Intelligence</title><meta name="description" content="Tirzepatide vs semaglutide in published head-to-head trials for weight and diabetes. Separate studies are not direct comparisons. Not medical advice."><link rel="canonical" href="https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide"><meta property="og:title" content="Tirzepatide vs Semaglutide: What Head-to-Head Trials Show"><meta property="og:description" content="Tirzepatide vs semaglutide in published head-to-head trials for weight and diabetes. Separate studies are not direct comparisons. Not medical advice."><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="og:url" content="https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Supplement Intelligence"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "Article", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide#article", "headline": "Tirzepatide vs Semaglutide: What Head-to-Head Trials Show", "alternativeHeadline": "Tirzepatide vs semaglutide: what the trials actually show", "description": "Tirzepatide vs semaglutide in published head-to-head trials for weight and diabetes. Separate studies are not direct comparisons. Not medical advice.", "datePublished": "2026-11-11", "dateModified": "2026-11-11", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide", "mainEntityOfPage": {"@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide"}, "inLanguage": "en-US", "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Supplement Intelligence", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/"}, "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Supplement Intelligence", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/"}, "about": ["Tirzepatide vs semaglutide: what the trials actually show"]}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide#faq", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/tirzepatide-vs-semaglutide", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Which caused more weight loss in a head-to-head trial?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Tirzepatide did in SURMOUNT-5. At 72 weeks, average weight change was −20.2% with tirzepatide versus −13.7% with semaglutide in adults with obesity without type 2 diabetes. These are trial averages, not individual predictions. [PMID 40353578]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How do they compare for type 2 diabetes?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Tirzepatide lowered HbA1c more than semaglutide 1 mg in SURPASS-2. HbA1c changes were −2.01, −2.24, and −2.30 percentage points with the studied tirzepatide groups versus −1.86 with semaglutide. [PMID 34170647]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is SURPASS-2 a comparison of the obesity doses?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. SURPASS-2 used semaglutide 1 mg, a type 2 diabetes dose, rather than the 2.4 mg obesity dose. It does not compare the obesity dose regimens. [PMID 34170647]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do they have the same effect on heart outcomes?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "That is not established by a head-to-head heart-outcomes trial in the evidence reviewed. SELECT compared semaglutide with placebo; SURPASS-CVOT compared tirzepatide with dulaglutide. Different comparators and populations prevent a direct ranking. [PMIDs 37952131, 41406444]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What side effects were reported in the head-to-head trials?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Stomach and bowel problems were the most common adverse events in SURMOUNT-5 and were mostly mild to moderate, mainly during dose escalation. SURPASS-2 also reported nausea, diarrhea, and vomiting, primarily mild to moderate. [PMIDs 40353578, 34170647]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Are tirzepatide and semaglutide the same type of drug?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "They overlap but are different. Tirzepatide activates GIP and GLP-1 receptors; semaglutide activates GLP-1 receptors only. Receptor activity alone does not establish which medicine suits an individual. [Zepbound, Mounjaro, and Wegovy labels]"}}]}]}</script></head><body><p><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/glp-1">GLP-1</a> / Tirzepatide vs semaglutide: what the trials actually show</p><h1>Tirzepatide vs semaglutide: what the trials actually show</h1><p>Evidence reviewed: November 11, 2026.</p><div class="si-verdict"><p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Published head-to-head trials found greater average weight loss with tirzepatide and greater HbA1c lowering versus semaglutide 1 mg. Those results do not settle every outcome or provide a personal treatment ranking. This page is not medical advice.</p></div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p><strong>Which caused more weight loss in a head-to-head trial?</strong><br>Tirzepatide did in SURMOUNT-5. At 72 weeks, average weight change was −20.2% with tirzepatide versus −13.7% with semaglutide in adults with obesity without type 2 diabetes. These are trial averages, not individual predictions. [PMID 40353578]</p><p><strong>How do they compare for type 2 diabetes?</strong><br>Tirzepatide lowered HbA1c more than semaglutide 1 mg in SURPASS-2. HbA1c changes were −2.01, −2.24, and −2.30 percentage points with the studied tirzepatide groups versus −1.86 with semaglutide. [PMID 34170647]</p><p><strong>Is SURPASS-2 a comparison of the obesity doses?</strong><br>No. SURPASS-2 used semaglutide 1 mg, a type 2 diabetes dose, rather than the 2.4 mg obesity dose. It does not compare the obesity dose regimens. [PMID 34170647]</p><p><strong>Do they have the same effect on heart outcomes?</strong><br>That is not established by a head-to-head heart-outcomes trial in the evidence reviewed. SELECT compared semaglutide with placebo; SURPASS-CVOT compared tirzepatide with dulaglutide. Different comparators and populations prevent a direct ranking. [PMIDs 37952131, 41406444]</p><p><strong>What side effects were reported in the head-to-head trials?</strong><br>Stomach and bowel problems were the most common adverse events in SURMOUNT-5 and were mostly mild to moderate, mainly during dose escalation. SURPASS-2 also reported nausea, diarrhea, and vomiting, primarily mild to moderate. [PMIDs 40353578, 34170647]</p><p><strong>Are tirzepatide and semaglutide the same type of drug?</strong><br>They overlap but are different. Tirzepatide activates GIP and GLP-1 receptors; semaglutide activates GLP-1 receptors only. Receptor activity alone does not establish which medicine suits an individual. [Zepbound, Mounjaro, and Wegovy labels]</p><h2>How to read the evidence</h2><ul><li><strong>Grade A:</strong> a published Phase 3 randomized trial, or for nutrient pages a published systematic review of randomized trials, for outcomes reported within that evidence.</li><li><strong>Grade B:</strong> a Phase 2 trial, exploratory or subgroup analysis, small human trial, observational study, or labeling statement beyond a trial abstract.</li><li><strong>Grade C:</strong> uncontrolled reports, animal or laboratory inference, mechanism-based predictions, and comparisons between separate trials.</li></ul><h2>What the medicines are</h2><p>Tirzepatide activates GIP and GLP-1 receptors. Semaglutide activates GLP-1 receptors only. Brand indications cannot be exchanged.</p><h2>Weight loss: SURMOUNT-5</h2><p>This open-label Phase 3b trial enrolled 751 adults with obesity without type 2 diabetes for 72 weeks. <strong>Grade A.</strong></p><div class="si-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Tirzepatide</th><th>Semaglutide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Average weight change</td><td>−20.2%</td><td>−13.7%</td></tr><tr><td>Waist change</td><td>−18.4 cm</td><td>−13.0 cm</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Participants and clinicians knew the assigned medicine. Eli Lilly funded the trial. People with type 2 diabetes were not enrolled.</p><h2>Blood sugar: SURPASS-2</h2><p>This open-label Phase 3 trial randomized 1,879 adults with type 2 diabetes for 40 weeks. <strong>Grade A within this comparison.</strong></p><div class="si-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>HbA1c change</th><th>Weight difference vs semaglutide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tirzepatide 5 mg</td><td>−2.01</td><td>−1.9 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Tirzepatide 10 mg</td><td>−2.24</td><td>−3.6 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Tirzepatide 15 mg</td><td>−2.30</td><td>−5.5 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Semaglutide 1 mg</td><td>−1.86</td><td>Comparator</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Side effects within direct comparisons</h3><p>Gastrointestinal events were most common and mainly mild to moderate. Both labels carry a boxed warning about thyroid C-cell tumors in rodents; human relevance is unknown.</p><h2>What separate studies add—and cannot settle</h2><p>SURMOUNT-1 and STEP 1 each compared a drug with placebo. Those are Grade A placebo results. Using separate trials to declare a winner is Grade C because populations, duration, and methods differ. A matched electronic-health-record cohort favored tirzepatide but does not become randomized evidence. <strong>Grade B.</strong></p><h2>Heart outcomes do not have a head-to-head winner here</h2><p>SELECT compared semaglutide with placebo and found a hazard ratio of 0.80 for cardiovascular death, nonfatal heart attack, or nonfatal stroke. SURPASS-CVOT compared tirzepatide with dulaglutide and established noninferiority, not superiority. Neither was a tirzepatide-versus-semaglutide heart-outcomes trial.</p><h2>Where retatrutide fits</h2><p><a href="/glp-1/retatrutide">Retatrutide</a> is a separate investigational medicine. Results from separate drug trials do not create a direct comparison.</p><h2>Primary sources</h2><ol class="si-sources"><li>Aronne LJ et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40353578/">Tirzepatide as Compared with Semaglutide for the Treatment of Obesity</a>. PMID 40353578.</li><li>Frías JP et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34170647/">Tirzepatide versus Semaglutide Once Weekly in Patients with Type 2 Diabetes</a>. PMID 34170647.</li><li>Lincoff AM et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37952131/">Semaglutide and Cardiovascular Outcomes</a>. PMID 37952131.</li><li>Nicholls SJ et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406444/">Cardiovascular Outcomes with Tirzepatide versus Dulaglutide</a>. PMID 41406444.</li></ol><h2>Related evidence</h2><ul><li><a href="/ingredients/creatine-monohydrate">Creatine monohydrate evidence</a></li><li><a href="/ingredients/magnesium">Magnesium evidence</a></li><li><a href="/glp-1">GLP-1 evidence guides</a></li><li><a href="/references">How we handle references</a></li><li><a href="/about">About Supplement Intelligence</a></li></ul><p>This page summarizes research and label text. It is not medical advice or a recommendation to start, stop, or change a medicine or supplement. Study protocols are not personal dosing instructions.</p></body></html>
