+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Creatine HCl vs Monohydrate: What Studies Compare | Supplement Intelligence</title><meta name="description" content="Human head-to-head trials of creatine hydrochloride and monohydrate are small and mixed. What they found, and what they did not settle. Not medical advice."><link rel="canonical" href="https://supplement-intelligence.com/ingredients/creatine-hcl-vs-monohydrate"><meta property="og:title" content="Creatine HCl vs Monohydrate: What Studies Compare"><meta property="og:description" content="Human head-to-head trials of creatine hydrochloride and monohydrate are small and mixed. What they found, and what they did not settle. Not medical advice."><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="og:url" content="https://supplement-intelligence.com/ingredients/creatine-hcl-vs-monohydrate"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Supplement Intelligence"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "Article", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/ingredients/creatine-hcl-vs-monohydrate#article", "headline": "Creatine HCl vs Monohydrate: What Studies Compare", "alternativeHeadline": "Creatine HCl vs monohydrate: what studies actually compare", "description": "Human head-to-head trials of creatine hydrochloride and monohydrate are small and mixed. What they found, and what they did not settle. Not medical advice.", "datePublished": "2026-11-11", "dateModified": "2026-11-11", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/ingredients/creatine-hcl-vs-monohydrate", "mainEntityOfPage": {"@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/ingredients/creatine-hcl-vs-monohydrate"}, "inLanguage": "en-US", "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Supplement Intelligence", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/"}, "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Supplement Intelligence", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/"}, "about": ["Creatine HCl vs monohydrate: what studies actually compare"]}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/ingredients/creatine-hcl-vs-monohydrate#faq", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/ingredients/creatine-hcl-vs-monohydrate", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Is creatine HCl better than monohydrate?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "That is not established. An 8-week resistance-training trial found both forms beat placebo for strength and muscle-size measures, with no benefit of HCl over monohydrate. [PMID 39545789]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Did any study find HCl was better?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A 4-week soccer study reported greater short-term physical-performance and peak-power changes with ethyl ester and HCl than with monohydrate. That trial was small (10 people per group) and short, and it conflicts with the 8-week training comparison. [PMID 42720250]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does HCl work at a lower amount?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Claims that HCl works at a much lower amount are not settled here. The 8-week trial used 0.03 g/kg of HCl, and compared it with monohydrate either loaded or used at 0.03 g/kg. Both monohydrate approaches and HCl outperformed placebo, without an HCl advantage. [PMID 39545789]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does HCl cause less bloating?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The evidence reviewed does not include a clear head-to-head bloating or stomach-tolerance result that establishes HCl as better tolerated."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Are there many independent comparisons?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Human head-to-head evidence remains limited to small, short trials, and the two main comparisons do not agree."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Which form has more research overall?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Creatine monohydrate has a much larger research base, including lean-mass reviews. A larger monohydrate evidence base is not the same as a head-to-head win. [PMID 35986981]"}}]}]}</script></head><body><p><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/ingredients">Ingredients</a> / Creatine HCl vs monohydrate: what studies actually compare</p><h1>Creatine HCl vs monohydrate: what studies actually compare</h1><p>Evidence reviewed: November 11, 2026.</p><div class="si-verdict"><p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Human head-to-head evidence for creatine HCl versus monohydrate is small and mixed, so a winner is not established. Claims that HCl works at a much lower amount or causes less bloating are not settled by these trials. Monohydrate has a much larger overall research base, which is a separate point from winning a direct comparison.</p></div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p><strong>Is creatine HCl better than monohydrate?</strong><br>That is not established. An 8-week resistance-training trial found both forms beat placebo for strength and muscle-size measures, with no benefit of HCl over monohydrate. [PMID 39545789]</p><p><strong>Did any study find HCl was better?</strong><br>A 4-week soccer study reported greater short-term physical-performance and peak-power changes with ethyl ester and HCl than with monohydrate. That trial was small (10 people per group) and short, and it conflicts with the 8-week training comparison. [PMID 42720250]</p><p><strong>Does HCl work at a lower amount?</strong><br>Claims that HCl works at a much lower amount are not settled here. The 8-week trial used 0.03 g/kg of HCl, and compared it with monohydrate either loaded or used at 0.03 g/kg. Both monohydrate approaches and HCl outperformed placebo, without an HCl advantage. [PMID 39545789]</p><p><strong>Does HCl cause less bloating?</strong><br>The evidence reviewed does not include a clear head-to-head bloating or stomach-tolerance result that establishes HCl as better tolerated.</p><p><strong>Are there many independent comparisons?</strong><br>No. Human head-to-head evidence remains limited to small, short trials, and the two main comparisons do not agree.</p><p><strong>Which form has more research overall?</strong><br>Creatine monohydrate has a much larger research base, including lean-mass reviews. A larger monohydrate evidence base is not the same as a head-to-head win. [PMID 35986981]</p><h2>How to read the evidence</h2><ul><li><strong>Grade A:</strong> a published Phase 3 randomized trial, or for nutrient pages a published systematic review of randomized trials, for outcomes reported within that evidence.</li><li><strong>Grade B:</strong> a Phase 2 trial, exploratory or subgroup analysis, small human trial, observational study, or labeling statement beyond a trial abstract.</li><li><strong>Grade C:</strong> uncontrolled reports, animal or laboratory inference, mechanism-based predictions, and comparisons between separate trials.</li></ul><h2>The eight-week resistance-training comparison</h2><p>Eghbali, Arazi, and Suzuki randomized 40 participants aged 18–25 into four groups of 10. The study used HCl at 0.03 g/kg, monohydrate with a loading period followed by 0.03 g/kg, monohydrate at 0.03 g/kg without loading, or placebo, alongside eight weeks of resistance training. These are study protocols, not instructions. <strong>Grade B.</strong></p><p>Supplementation groups improved strength, arm and thigh muscle cross-sectional area, skeletal muscle mass, and percent body fat compared with placebo. The authors found no benefit of HCl over monohydrate.</p><h2>The four-week soccer comparison found a different result</h2><p>Duan and colleagues randomized 40 collegiate male soccer players into monohydrate, ethyl ester, HCl, or placebo groups, with 10 per group, during four weeks of repeated-sprint training. <strong>Grade B.</strong></p><p>All training groups improved. Creatine groups had greater changes than placebo, and ethyl ester and HCl showed greater physical-performance and peak-power gains than monohydrate. There were no significant group differences for mean power or VO2max.</p><p>The small groups, short duration, male soccer population, and different outcomes limit interpretation. This conflicts with the eight-week comparison and does not establish broader superiority.</p><h2>What remains untested here</h2><p>The comparisons do not provide a clear head-to-head bloating or stomach-tolerance result. A claim of better tolerance needs its own evidence. A 35-study, 1,192-participant lean-mass review gives context but does not establish HCl-versus-monohydrate superiority.</p><h2>Bottom line</h2><p>Both direct comparisons are small and short, and their findings differ. Monohydrate has more research overall, but these trials do not settle a universal winner, a lower-amount HCl advantage, or a bloating advantage.</p><h2>Primary sources</h2><ol class="si-sources"><li>Eghbali E, Arazi H, Suzuki K. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39545789/">Supplementing With Which Form of Creatine?</a> Physiol Res. 2024. PMID 39545789.</li><li>Duan C, Wang Z, Wang Q. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42720250/">Creatine formulations and repeated sprint training</a>. 2026. PMID 42720250.</li><li>Delpino FM et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35986981/">Influence of age, sex, and type of exercise on creatine efficacy</a>. PMID 35986981.</li></ol><h2>Related evidence</h2><ul><li><a href="/ingredients/creatine-monohydrate">Creatine monohydrate evidence</a></li><li><a href="/ingredients/magnesium">Magnesium evidence</a></li><li><a href="/glp-1">GLP-1 evidence guides</a></li><li><a href="/references">How we handle references</a></li><li><a href="/about">About Supplement Intelligence</a></li></ul><p>This page summarizes research and label text. It is not medical advice or a recommendation to start, stop, or change a medicine or supplement. Study protocols are not personal dosing instructions.</p></body></html>
